<?xml version="1.0" ?>
<!--
  forklift_arena.sdf . M4 forklift commissioning arena for the amr-agent
  project (ADR 0008).

  A bare hall for driving, steering and lifting the in house forklift
  (agv/forklift/model.sdf). No vehicle is embedded here: the model is a
  plain <model> and is spawned into this world by
  sim/launch/forklift_bringup.launch.py, so the arena and the vehicle stay
  separately owned.

  THE FIXED EQUIPMENT DEMO CELL IS NOT EMBEDDED. sim/worlds/cell.sdf keeps
  its conveyor, photo eye and operator panel, and this file has none of
  them. The coupled cell plus vehicle scenario is roadmap M6 work (AT 07
  coupled, a vehicle in the monitored zone; ADR 0010) and belongs to that
  gate, not to this one. Neither file includes the other.

  NO CONTROL LOGIC LIVES HERE. There is no sequencing, no interlock, no
  timer, no latch, no threshold and no zone decision in this file. The
  props below are geometry: they stand still and the vehicle scanner
  measures whatever range they present. Deciding what a range means is the
  PLC standard program's job under ADR 0008 D3 (invariants 5, 6 and 9).

  NOTHING HERE IS A SAFETY DEVICE. The obstacle props are process
  furniture. The protective stop, the e stop chain and safe torque off are
  onboard and hardwired and appear in no world file (invariant 1).

  ==================== LAYOUT (top view, metres) ====================

  24.0 x 16.0 floor, origin at the centre of the hall. Perimeter walls are
  0.60 m high so the vehicle scanner, which sweeps a horizontal plane at
  z = 0.25 in the vehicle frame, terminates on them instead of running out
  to its 8.0 m range maximum.

              +y            north wall, inner face y = +7.90
      .........+.................................................
      .        |  [PalletZone 2.4 x 2.0 marking]                .
      .        |   [Pallet + LoadBox] at (-7.50, +4.50)         .
      .        |            [CrateNorth] (-4.50, +4.20)         .
      . . . . .+. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .  aisle edge y = +2.00
      .        |                                                .
      .        |  [spawn (-6.00, 0)] >>>      [AisleCrate]      .
   ===+========+=============== drive aisle, centreline y = 0 =======> +x
      .        |                       straddling it at x = 2.00 .
      . . . . .+. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .  aisle edge y = -2.00
      .        |     [PillarSouth] (-2.00, -3.20)               .
      .        |                                                .
      .........+.................................................
        west wall            south wall, inner face y = -7.90
        x = -11.90                                    east wall x = +11.90

  ==================== WHAT THE SCANNER SEES ====================

  The forklift scanner sits at z = 0.25 in the vehicle frame and sweeps a
  single horizontal plane, so height decides visibility:

    seen      perimeter walls (0 to 0.60), AisleCrate (0 to 0.90),
              PillarSouth (0 to 2.60), CrateNorth (0 to 1.00),
              LoadBox (0.16 to 0.86)
    not seen  the floor markings (0.006 thick, lying on the floor) and
              the Pallet itself, whose deck tops out at z = 0.16

  A pallet that the scanner cannot see while the load on it can is the
  real geometry of a low pallet under a truck mounted scanner, not an
  oversight. It is written down here because it is the kind of thing that
  otherwise gets discovered as a bug.

  ==================== THE STOP ZONE PROP ====================

  AisleCrate is placed for the obstacle scenario of the M4 gate criterion.
  It stands ON the aisle centreline at x = 2.00 with its front face square
  to the aisle at x = 1.55, so a vehicle driving straight up the aisle
  meets it head on and the scenario is repeatable without steering. It
  spans y = -0.45 to +0.45, leaving 1.55 m of the marked aisle clear on
  each side of it.

  Placing it off the centreline was tried and rejected on arithmetic, not
  taste. The stop zone is the +-30 deg sector of a scanner whose process
  stop distance is 1.20 m, so a prop whose near face is more than
  1.20 * sin(30 deg) = 0.60 m off the driving line can never be inside both
  the sector and the distance at once: it leaves the sector as the vehicle
  closes on it, and the in sector range asymptotes to (offset / sin 30 deg)
  instead of falling to the threshold. At the 1.55 m offset an aisle edge
  placement would need, the nearest in sector range is 3.10 m and the zone
  never trips.

  Where the vehicle is when the zone trips, for reference only. The scanner
  leads the model origin by 0.72 m and the process stop distance in
  agv/forklift/config.yaml is 1.20 m, so a range of 1.20 m to the crate
  face puts the model origin at

      1.55 - 1.20 - 0.72 = -0.37

  which is 5.63 m of travel from the spawn pose the bringup launch
  defaults to. That threshold is a config.yaml value evaluated by a vehicle
  node, and later by the PLC; this world neither knows it nor enforces it.

  ==================== SENSOR SYSTEM ====================

  The vehicle scanner is a gz "gpu_lidar", so this world MUST load
  gz-sim-sensors-system with a render engine, exactly as cell.sdf does. gz's
  stock empty.sdf does not, and on it the vehicle drives and lifts while
  /forklift/gz/scan stays silent (agv/forklift/README.md).

  Rendering on the target machine is llvmpipe software rasterisation
  (sim/setup/WSL_ENVIRONMENT.md), so the scene is deliberately cheap: box
  and cylinder primitives only, no mesh, no texture, no shadow, one
  directional light. Adding any of those is a change to the figures in
  worlds/FORKLIFT_ARENA_EVIDENCE.md and has to be re measured.
-->
<sdf version="1.8">
  <world name="forklift_arena">

    <!-- Same 2 ms fixed step as cell.sdf and as the throwaway world the
         model was verified on (agv/forklift/EVIDENCE_MODEL.md section 0),
         so joint rates measured here are comparable with the ones measured
         there. The hall is empty enough that 500 Hz still runs at real
         time headless. -->
    <physics type="ode">
      <max_step_size>0.002</max_step_size>
      <real_time_factor>1.0</real_time_factor>
      <real_time_update_rate>500</real_time_update_rate>
    </physics>

    <plugin name="gz::sim::systems::Physics" filename="libgz-sim-physics-system.so"/>
    <plugin name="gz::sim::systems::UserCommands" filename="libgz-sim-user-commands-system.so"/>
    <plugin name="gz::sim::systems::SceneBroadcaster" filename="libgz-sim-scene-broadcaster-system.so"/>

    <!-- Required by the vehicle's gpu_lidar. Without this the scan topic
         is never advertised and the failure is silent. -->
    <plugin name="gz::sim::systems::Sensors" filename="libgz-sim-sensors-system.so">
      <render_engine>ogre2</render_engine>
    </plugin>

    <!-- ======================= GUI CONFIGURATION ====================== -->
    <!--
      THIS BLOCK IS WHY THE LIDAR BEAMS ARE VISIBLE. Without it they are
      not, however the sensors are declared.

      In Gazebo Harmonic `<visualize>true</visualize>` on a sensor is
      NECESSARY BUT NOT SUFFICIENT. The plugin that draws laser scans is
      VisualizeLidar; it is not part of gz sim's default GUI
      configuration, it never reads the sensor's visualize flag, and it
      finds its data by listing gz transport topics that advertise
      gz.msgs.LaserScan. Loading it is this block's whole job. Every
      Gazebo Classic tutorial says the sensor flag is enough; on Harmonic
      it is not, and the failure is silent, because the sensor publishes
      correct scans the whole time the screen shows nothing.

      Reference followed: gz sim's own examples/worlds/visualize_lidar.sdf,
      which on the verified container is at
      /opt/ros/jazzy/opt/gz_sim_vendor/share/gz/gz-sim8/worlds/visualize_lidar.sdf
      (gz sim 8.11.0).

      HOW THE PLUGIN CHOOSES A SCANNER, because it decides what a viewer
      sees. VisualizeLidar draws ONE topic at a time, picked in its own
      combo box, whose entries are the LaserScan topics live at the moment
      its refresh button was last pressed, in the order gz transport lists
      them. The vehicle carries three, so a viewer who wants the
      navigation lidar selects /forklift/gz/scan_nav there; the safety
      pair is on /forklift/gz/safety_scanner_front/measurement and
      /forklift/gz/safety_scanner_rear/measurement. The GUI cannot show
      all three at once and this file cannot preselect one: the plugin
      takes no topic parameter from SDF.

      Display type is the plugin's second combo box, defaulting to
      Triangle Strips. Rays draws one line segment per sample per frame,
      which is the mode a viewer means by "beams" and the most expensive
      of the three on a software rasteriser. The measured cost of all of
      this is in worlds/FORKLIFT_ARENA_EVIDENCE.md section 9; it was
      measured, not assumed, because rendering here is llvmpipe.

      The scene settings below are repeated from the world's <scene>
      element on purpose: MinimalScene does not inherit them, it carries
      its own copies, and letting the two disagree gives a GUI that looks
      nothing like the headless render the sensors see.

      DELIBERATELY ABSENT: ComponentInspector and EntityTree. They are in
      the gz reference and are not here, because every extra GUI plugin
      costs frames on a software rasteriser and neither is needed to see a
      beam. Adding one is a change to the section 9 figures.
    -->
    <gui fullscreen="0">

      <plugin filename="MinimalScene" name="3D View">
        <gz-gui>
          <title>3D View</title>
          <property type="bool" key="showTitleBar">false</property>
          <property type="string" key="state">docked</property>
        </gz-gui>
        <engine>ogre2</engine>
        <scene>scene</scene>
        <ambient_light>1.0 1.0 1.0</ambient_light>
        <background_color>0.7 0.7 0.7</background_color>
        <!-- Looking north east from over the south west corner of the
             hall, so the default spawn pose at (-6.00, 0) and the aisle
             ahead of it are both in frame with no camera move. -->
        <camera_pose>-13.5 -8.5 5.5 0 0.36 0.84</camera_pose>
      </plugin>

      <!-- Feeds the 3D view from the server's scene topic. Without it the
           view renders an empty scene and every other plugin here has
           nothing to attach to. -->
      <plugin filename="GzSceneManager" name="Scene Manager">
        <gz-gui>
          <property key="resizable" type="bool">false</property>
          <property key="width" type="double">5</property>
          <property key="height" type="double">5</property>
          <property key="state" type="string">floating</property>
          <property key="showTitleBar" type="bool">false</property>
        </gz-gui>
      </plugin>

      <plugin filename="InteractiveViewControl" name="Interactive view control">
        <gz-gui>
          <property key="resizable" type="bool">false</property>
          <property key="width" type="double">5</property>
          <property key="height" type="double">5</property>
          <property key="state" type="string">floating</property>
          <property key="showTitleBar" type="bool">false</property>
        </gz-gui>
      </plugin>

      <!-- Camera follow. With EntityContextMenuPlugin below it is what
           lets a viewer right click the vehicle and ride with it while
           the beams sweep. -->
      <plugin filename="CameraTracking" name="Camera Tracking">
        <gz-gui>
          <property key="resizable" type="bool">false</property>
          <property key="width" type="double">5</property>
          <property key="height" type="double">5</property>
          <property key="state" type="string">floating</property>
          <property key="showTitleBar" type="bool">false</property>
        </gz-gui>
      </plugin>

      <plugin filename="EntityContextMenuPlugin" name="Entity context menu">
        <gz-gui>
          <property key="state" type="string">floating</property>
          <property key="width" type="double">5</property>
          <property key="height" type="double">5</property>
          <property key="showTitleBar" type="bool">false</property>
        </gz-gui>
      </plugin>

      <!-- NOT start_paused. The scanners are always_on but the sensors
           system renders on simulation steps, so a paused world publishes
           no scan, the plugin's topic list comes up empty and the beams
           never appear. The arena is launched with -r for the same
           reason. -->
      <plugin filename="WorldControl" name="World control">
        <gz-gui>
          <title>World control</title>
          <property type="bool" key="showTitleBar">false</property>
          <property type="bool" key="resizable">false</property>
          <property type="double" key="height">72</property>
          <property type="double" key="z">1</property>
          <property type="string" key="state">floating</property>
          <anchors target="3D View">
            <line own="left" target="left"/>
            <line own="bottom" target="bottom"/>
          </anchors>
        </gz-gui>
        <play_pause>true</play_pause>
        <step>true</step>
        <start_paused>false</start_paused>
        <use_event>true</use_event>
      </plugin>

      <!-- The real time factor readout. It is the instrument the render
           budget in FORKLIFT_ARENA_EVIDENCE.md section 9 is read from,
           so it stays on screen during any recording. -->
      <plugin filename="WorldStats" name="World stats">
        <gz-gui>
          <title>World stats</title>
          <property type="bool" key="showTitleBar">false</property>
          <property type="bool" key="resizable">false</property>
          <property type="double" key="height">110</property>
          <property type="double" key="width">290</property>
          <property type="double" key="z">1</property>
          <property type="string" key="state">floating</property>
          <anchors target="3D View">
            <line own="right" target="right"/>
            <line own="bottom" target="bottom"/>
          </anchors>
        </gz-gui>
        <sim_time>true</sim_time>
        <real_time>true</real_time>
        <real_time_factor>true</real_time_factor>
        <iterations>true</iterations>
      </plugin>

      <!-- The beams. Docked rather than floating so the topic and
           display type combo boxes are visible without hunting: which
           scanner is on screen is not guessable from the picture. -->
      <plugin filename="VisualizeLidar" name="Visualize Lidar">
        <gz-gui>
          <title>Visualize lidar</title>
          <property type="string" key="state">docked</property>
        </gz-gui>
      </plugin>

      <!-- Capture. Writes a PNG of the 3D view and also advertises the
           /gui/screenshot service, which is how the beam capture in
           FORKLIFT_ARENA_EVIDENCE.md section 9 was taken without a human
           at the window. -->
      <plugin filename="Screenshot" name="Screenshot">
        <gz-gui>
          <property type="bool" key="showTitleBar">false</property>
          <property type="string" key="state">floating</property>
          <property type="double" key="width">100</property>
          <property type="double" key="height">50</property>
        </gz-gui>
      </plugin>

    </gui>

    <scene>
      <ambient>1 1 1 1</ambient>
      <background>0.7 0.7 0.7 1</background>
      <shadows>false</shadows>
      <grid>false</grid>
    </scene>

    <light name="sun" type="directional">
      <cast_shadows>false</cast_shadows>
      <pose>0 0 12 0 0 0</pose>
      <diffuse>1.0 1.0 1.0 1</diffuse>
      <specular>0.2 0.2 0.2 1</specular>
      <direction>-0.3 0.3 -1.0</direction>
    </light>

    <gravity>0 0 -9.8</gravity>

    <!-- ============================ Floor ============================ -->
    <!-- Plain concrete. mu 1.0 so the drive wheel has something to push
         against; the tricycle is a single driven wheel and a slippery
         floor turns traction figures into tyre slip figures. -->
    <model name="Floor">
      <static>true</static>
      <link name="link">
        <collision name="collision">
          <geometry>
            <plane><normal>0 0 1</normal><size>24 16</size></plane>
          </geometry>
          <surface>
            <friction><ode><mu>1.0</mu><mu2>1.0</mu2></ode></friction>
          </surface>
        </collision>
        <visual name="visual">
          <geometry>
            <plane><normal>0 0 1</normal><size>24 16</size></plane>
          </geometry>
          <material>
            <ambient>0.55 0.55 0.55 1</ambient>
            <diffuse>0.55 0.55 0.55 1</diffuse>
          </material>
        </visual>
      </link>
    </model>

    <!-- ======================= Perimeter walls ======================= -->
    <!--
      Four low walls, 0.20 thick and 0.60 high, enclosing 23.8 x 15.8 of
      clear floor. Low on purpose: they bound the arena and give the
      scanner a real return without adding the render cost of full height
      warehouse walls. The north and south walls run the full 24.4 m so the
      corners close.
    -->
    <model name="WallWest">
      <static>true</static>
      <pose>-12.00 0 0.30 0 0 0</pose>
      <link name="link">
        <collision name="collision">
          <geometry><box><size>0.20 16.00 0.60</size></box></geometry>
        </collision>
        <visual name="visual">
          <geometry><box><size>0.20 16.00 0.60</size></box></geometry>
          <material><ambient>0.62 0.62 0.64 1</ambient><diffuse>0.62 0.62 0.64 1</diffuse></material>
        </visual>
      </link>
    </model>

    <model name="WallEast">
      <static>true</static>
      <pose>12.00 0 0.30 0 0 0</pose>
      <link name="link">
        <collision name="collision">
          <geometry><box><size>0.20 16.00 0.60</size></box></geometry>
        </collision>
        <visual name="visual">
          <geometry><box><size>0.20 16.00 0.60</size></box></geometry>
          <material><ambient>0.62 0.62 0.64 1</ambient><diffuse>0.62 0.62 0.64 1</diffuse></material>
        </visual>
      </link>
    </model>

    <model name="WallSouth">
      <static>true</static>
      <pose>0 -8.00 0.30 0 0 0</pose>
      <link name="link">
        <collision name="collision">
          <geometry><box><size>24.40 0.20 0.60</size></box></geometry>
        </collision>
        <visual name="visual">
          <geometry><box><size>24.40 0.20 0.60</size></box></geometry>
          <material><ambient>0.62 0.62 0.64 1</ambient><diffuse>0.62 0.62 0.64 1</diffuse></material>
        </visual>
      </link>
    </model>

    <model name="WallNorth">
      <static>true</static>
      <pose>0 8.00 0.30 0 0 0</pose>
      <link name="link">
        <collision name="collision">
          <geometry><box><size>24.40 0.20 0.60</size></box></geometry>
        </collision>
        <visual name="visual">
          <geometry><box><size>24.40 0.20 0.60</size></box></geometry>
          <material><ambient>0.62 0.62 0.64 1</ambient><diffuse>0.62 0.62 0.64 1</diffuse></material>
        </visual>
      </link>
    </model>

    <!-- ====================== Drive aisle marking ===================== -->
    <!--
      Two painted edge lines at y = +-2.00 defining a 4.0 m clear aisle
      along the hall. VISUAL ONLY: no collision, 6 mm proud of the floor,
      so nothing drives over a kerb and the horizontal scanner plane at
      z = 0.25 never sees them. Paint is not equipment; it marks where the
      aisle is for a human watching the run.
    -->
    <model name="AisleMarking">
      <static>true</static>
      <link name="link">
        <visual name="edge_north">
          <pose>0 2.00 0.003 0 0 0</pose>
          <geometry><box><size>21.00 0.10 0.006</size></box></geometry>
          <material><ambient>0.92 0.80 0.16 1</ambient><diffuse>0.92 0.80 0.16 1</diffuse></material>
        </visual>
        <visual name="edge_south">
          <pose>0 -2.00 0.003 0 0 0</pose>
          <geometry><box><size>21.00 0.10 0.006</size></box></geometry>
          <material><ambient>0.92 0.80 0.16 1</ambient><diffuse>0.92 0.80 0.16 1</diffuse></material>
        </visual>
      </link>
    </model>

    <!-- ======================= Pallet zone marking ==================== -->
    <!--
      A 2.40 x 2.00 painted outline north of the aisle, marking where a
      pallet belongs. Same rules as the aisle paint: four visual only
      strips, no collision, invisible to the scanner plane.
    -->
    <model name="PalletZoneMarking">
      <static>true</static>
      <pose>-7.50 4.50 0 0 0 0</pose>
      <link name="link">
        <visual name="edge_north">
          <pose>0 0.95 0.003 0 0 0</pose>
          <geometry><box><size>2.40 0.10 0.006</size></box></geometry>
          <material><ambient>0.92 0.80 0.16 1</ambient><diffuse>0.92 0.80 0.16 1</diffuse></material>
        </visual>
        <visual name="edge_south">
          <pose>0 -0.95 0.003 0 0 0</pose>
          <geometry><box><size>2.40 0.10 0.006</size></box></geometry>
          <material><ambient>0.92 0.80 0.16 1</ambient><diffuse>0.92 0.80 0.16 1</diffuse></material>
        </visual>
        <visual name="edge_west">
          <pose>-1.15 0 0.003 0 0 0</pose>
          <geometry><box><size>0.10 1.80 0.006</size></box></geometry>
          <material><ambient>0.92 0.80 0.16 1</ambient><diffuse>0.92 0.80 0.16 1</diffuse></material>
        </visual>
        <visual name="edge_east">
          <pose>1.15 0 0.003 0 0 0</pose>
          <geometry><box><size>0.10 1.80 0.006</size></box></geometry>
          <material><ambient>0.92 0.80 0.16 1</ambient><diffuse>0.92 0.80 0.16 1</diffuse></material>
        </visual>
      </link>
    </model>

    <!-- =============== Safety zone marking (SF-07 pattern) =============== -->
    <!--
      A 3.00 x 3.00 painted outline on open floor along the drive aisle,
      west of AisleCrate and independent of it: 2.5 m of clear travel from
      the spawn pose (-6.00, 0) to this outline's near edge at x = -3.50,
      and a further 2.05 m of clear floor from its far edge at x = -0.50 to
      AisleCrate's near face at x = 1.55. Its north and south edges sit at
      y = +-1.45, 0.55 m inside the aisle edge lines at y = +-2.00. Same
      technique as AisleMarking and PalletZoneMarking above: four visual
      only strips, no collision, 6 mm proud of the floor, invisible to the
      horizontal scanner plane at z = 0.25.

      THIS IS PAINT, NOT A SENSOR. Nothing in this file watches whether the
      vehicle is inside this outline, and no crossing is detected here. The
      zone F-input this outline stands for is an engineering stand-in,
      latched by the F-CPU safety program from a value the owner writes at
      the TIA Portal watch table by hand, at the moment a viewer sees the
      model cross this line (plc/forklift-safety/SPEC.md section 7). That
      program implements the SF-07 pattern, never SF-07 itself, and never
      shares a tag, a node, a lamp or a sentence with the lidar obstacle
      stop that AisleCrate exercises a few metres east of here
      (plc/forklift-safety/SPEC.md section 1.3; docs/safety/TWIN-DEMO-MAP.md
      rule R4). Crossing this outline needs no interaction with AisleCrate
      and trips no lidar logic; the two scenarios are independent by
      placement, not by a rule enforced in code.

      Adding this model changes what `gz model --list` reports: thirteen
      arena models instead of twelve. worlds/FORKLIFT_ARENA_EVIDENCE.md was
      captured before this marking existed and is not re-measured here.
    -->
    <model name="SafetyZoneMarking">
      <static>true</static>
      <pose>-2.00 0.00 0 0 0 0</pose>
      <link name="link">
        <visual name="edge_north">
          <pose>0 1.45 0.003 0 0 0</pose>
          <geometry><box><size>3.00 0.10 0.006</size></box></geometry>
          <material><ambient>0.80 0.12 0.12 1</ambient><diffuse>0.80 0.12 0.12 1</diffuse></material>
        </visual>
        <visual name="edge_south">
          <pose>0 -1.45 0.003 0 0 0</pose>
          <geometry><box><size>3.00 0.10 0.006</size></box></geometry>
          <material><ambient>0.80 0.12 0.12 1</ambient><diffuse>0.80 0.12 0.12 1</diffuse></material>
        </visual>
        <visual name="edge_west">
          <pose>-1.45 0 0.003 0 0 0</pose>
          <geometry><box><size>0.10 2.80 0.006</size></box></geometry>
          <material><ambient>0.80 0.12 0.12 1</ambient><diffuse>0.80 0.12 0.12 1</diffuse></material>
        </visual>
        <visual name="edge_east">
          <pose>1.45 0 0.003 0 0 0</pose>
          <geometry><box><size>0.10 2.80 0.006</size></box></geometry>
          <material><ambient>0.80 0.12 0.12 1</ambient><diffuse>0.80 0.12 0.12 1</diffuse></material>
        </visual>
      </link>
    </model>

    <!-- ============================ Pallet =========================== -->
    <!--
      In house primitive pallet: three deck boards on two blocks, all in
      one rigid link. Five primitives, no mesh, nothing imported.

      Dimensions 1.20 (x) x 1.00 (y) x 0.16 (z). Fork entry is along x,
      which is the axis the blocks run on:

        blocks   y = +-0.45, 0.10 wide, so their inner faces sit at
                 y = +-0.40
        tines    y = +-0.28, 0.12 wide, so their outer faces sit at
                 y = +-0.34

      That is 0.06 m of clearance per side. Vertically the tine top is at
      z = 0.10 with the carriage fully lowered and the deck underside is at
      z = 0.12, so the forks enter under the deck with 0.02 m to spare and
      the load is picked by raising the carriage rather than by driving
      into it.

      Masses are chosen so a later lift stays inside the carriage
      controller's measured envelope, and the arithmetic is written here
      because it is not obvious. The mast controller's lifting integral
      clamp is i_min = -1500 (agv/forklift/model.sdf); the unloaded
      assembly weighs about 882 N, and pallet plus load add
      (20 + 30) * 9.8 = 490 N, for 1372 N against a 1500 N clamp.

      NOT MEASURED. Nothing was lifted in the run recorded in
      FORKLIFT_ARENA_EVIDENCE.md, and the m4f-02 fork tuning figures are
      for the unloaded carriage. The numbers above say the load is
      plausible, not that it works.
    -->
    <model name="Pallet">
      <static>false</static>
      <pose>-7.50 4.50 0 0 0 0</pose>
      <link name="link">
        <inertial>
          <pose>0 0 0.08 0 0 0</pose>
          <mass>20.0</mass>
          <inertia>
            <ixx>1.7093</ixx><ixy>0</ixy><ixz>0</ixz>
            <iyy>2.4427</iyy><iyz>0</iyz><izz>4.0667</izz>
          </inertia>
        </inertial>

        <collision name="block_left_collision">
          <pose>0 0.45 0.06 0 0 0</pose>
          <geometry><box><size>1.20 0.10 0.12</size></box></geometry>
          <surface>
            <friction><ode><mu>0.9</mu><mu2>0.9</mu2></ode></friction>
            <contact><ode><kp>1e6</kp><kd>100.0</kd></ode></contact>
          </surface>
        </collision>
        <visual name="block_left_visual">
          <pose>0 0.45 0.06 0 0 0</pose>
          <geometry><box><size>1.20 0.10 0.12</size></box></geometry>
          <material><ambient>0.66 0.50 0.30 1</ambient><diffuse>0.66 0.50 0.30 1</diffuse></material>
        </visual>

        <collision name="block_right_collision">
          <pose>0 -0.45 0.06 0 0 0</pose>
          <geometry><box><size>1.20 0.10 0.12</size></box></geometry>
          <surface>
            <friction><ode><mu>0.9</mu><mu2>0.9</mu2></ode></friction>
            <contact><ode><kp>1e6</kp><kd>100.0</kd></ode></contact>
          </surface>
        </collision>
        <visual name="block_right_visual">
          <pose>0 -0.45 0.06 0 0 0</pose>
          <geometry><box><size>1.20 0.10 0.12</size></box></geometry>
          <material><ambient>0.66 0.50 0.30 1</ambient><diffuse>0.66 0.50 0.30 1</diffuse></material>
        </visual>

        <collision name="deck_board_west_collision">
          <pose>-0.48 0 0.14 0 0 0</pose>
          <geometry><box><size>0.24 1.00 0.04</size></box></geometry>
          <surface>
            <friction><ode><mu>0.9</mu><mu2>0.9</mu2></ode></friction>
            <contact><ode><kp>1e6</kp><kd>100.0</kd></ode></contact>
          </surface>
        </collision>
        <visual name="deck_board_west_visual">
          <pose>-0.48 0 0.14 0 0 0</pose>
          <geometry><box><size>0.24 1.00 0.04</size></box></geometry>
          <material><ambient>0.72 0.56 0.34 1</ambient><diffuse>0.72 0.56 0.34 1</diffuse></material>
        </visual>

        <collision name="deck_board_mid_collision">
          <pose>0 0 0.14 0 0 0</pose>
          <geometry><box><size>0.24 1.00 0.04</size></box></geometry>
          <surface>
            <friction><ode><mu>0.9</mu><mu2>0.9</mu2></ode></friction>
            <contact><ode><kp>1e6</kp><kd>100.0</kd></ode></contact>
          </surface>
        </collision>
        <visual name="deck_board_mid_visual">
          <pose>0 0 0.14 0 0 0</pose>
          <geometry><box><size>0.24 1.00 0.04</size></box></geometry>
          <material><ambient>0.72 0.56 0.34 1</ambient><diffuse>0.72 0.56 0.34 1</diffuse></material>
        </visual>

        <collision name="deck_board_east_collision">
          <pose>0.48 0 0.14 0 0 0</pose>
          <geometry><box><size>0.24 1.00 0.04</size></box></geometry>
          <surface>
            <friction><ode><mu>0.9</mu><mu2>0.9</mu2></ode></friction>
            <contact><ode><kp>1e6</kp><kd>100.0</kd></ode></contact>
          </surface>
        </collision>
        <visual name="deck_board_east_visual">
          <pose>0.48 0 0.14 0 0 0</pose>
          <geometry><box><size>0.24 1.00 0.04</size></box></geometry>
          <material><ambient>0.72 0.56 0.34 1</ambient><diffuse>0.72 0.56 0.34 1</diffuse></material>
        </visual>
      </link>
    </model>

    <!-- =========================== Load box ========================== -->
    <!-- The load riding on the pallet. Bottom face sits on the deck top at
         z = 0.16, so it starts at rest and does not drop at world load. -->
    <model name="LoadBox">
      <pose>-7.50 4.50 0.51 0 0 0</pose>
      <link name="link">
        <inertial>
          <mass>30.0</mass>
          <inertia>
            <ixx>2.45</ixx><ixy>0</ixy><ixz>0</ixz>
            <iyy>2.45</iyy><iyz>0</iyz><izz>2.45</izz>
          </inertia>
        </inertial>
        <collision name="collision">
          <geometry><box><size>0.70 0.70 0.70</size></box></geometry>
          <surface>
            <friction><ode><mu>0.9</mu><mu2>0.9</mu2></ode></friction>
            <contact><ode><kp>1e6</kp><kd>100.0</kd></ode></contact>
          </surface>
        </collision>
        <visual name="visual">
          <geometry><box><size>0.70 0.70 0.70</size></box></geometry>
          <material><ambient>0.80 0.55 0.20 1</ambient><diffuse>0.80 0.55 0.20 1</diffuse></material>
        </visual>
      </link>
    </model>

    <!-- ========================= Obstacle props ======================= -->
    <!--
      Three static props. AisleCrate is the one placed for the stop zone
      scenario (see the header); the other two stand clear of the aisle so
      the scanner has something to return while the vehicle turns, and so
      that "the aisle is clear" is a statement with content.
    -->
    <model name="AisleCrate">
      <static>true</static>
      <pose>2.00 0.00 0.45 0 0 0</pose>
      <link name="link">
        <collision name="collision">
          <geometry><box><size>0.90 0.90 0.90</size></box></geometry>
        </collision>
        <visual name="visual">
          <geometry><box><size>0.90 0.90 0.90</size></box></geometry>
          <material><ambient>0.74 0.58 0.36 1</ambient><diffuse>0.74 0.58 0.36 1</diffuse></material>
        </visual>
      </link>
    </model>

    <model name="PillarSouth">
      <static>true</static>
      <pose>-2.00 -3.20 1.30 0 0 0</pose>
      <link name="link">
        <collision name="collision">
          <geometry><box><size>0.35 0.35 2.60</size></box></geometry>
        </collision>
        <visual name="visual">
          <geometry><box><size>0.35 0.35 2.60</size></box></geometry>
          <material><ambient>0.45 0.46 0.50 1</ambient><diffuse>0.45 0.46 0.50 1</diffuse></material>
        </visual>
      </link>
    </model>

    <model name="CrateNorth">
      <static>true</static>
      <pose>-4.50 4.20 0.50 0 0 0</pose>
      <link name="link">
        <collision name="collision">
          <geometry><box><size>1.20 0.80 1.00</size></box></geometry>
        </collision>
        <visual name="visual">
          <geometry><box><size>1.20 0.80 1.00</size></box></geometry>
          <material><ambient>0.74 0.58 0.36 1</ambient><diffuse>0.74 0.58 0.36 1</diffuse></material>
        </visual>
      </link>
    </model>

  </world>
</sdf>
